['Here is an abstract example of how you might start solving this problem in Java:

```java
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.*;
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Backoff;
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Retryable;
import org.springframework.web.client.RestClientException;

@Service
public class PokemonReportingService {

    private final ExternalApiClient apiClient;
    private final CacheManager cacheManager;

    public PokemonReportingService(ExternalApiClient apiClient, CacheManager cacheManager) {
        this.apiClient = apiClient;
        this.cacheManager = cacheManager;
    }

    @Retryable(value = {RestClientException.class}, maxAttempts = 3, backoff = @Backoff(delay = 1000))
    @Cacheable(value="pokemonReports", key="#id")
    public PokemonReport getPokemonReport(int id) {
        return apiClient.getPokemonReport(id);
    }

    public List<PokemonReport> getPokemonReportsBatch(List<Integer> ids) throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10); // 10 threads in the pool
        List<Future<PokemonReport>> futures = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int id : ids) {
            Future<PokemonReport> future = executor.submit(() -> getPokemonReport(id));
            futures.add(future);
        }

        List<PokemonReport> reports = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Future<PokemonReport> future : futures) {
            reports.add(future.get()); // get() will block until the result is ready
        }
        return reports;
    }
}
```

The ExternalApiClient would be an interface that uses Spring's RestTemplate or WebClient to interact with the external API. It should also include error handling and retry logic for failed requests. 

