["Here is a basic implementation of a semaphore in C language that uses mutex and condition variables from the POSIX thread library to handle threads synchronization and resource management. Note that you would need to adapt this code to your existing application framework, as well as adding error handling and logging functionalities. 

Please note that the semaphore doesn't actually integrate into a legacy API or an existing application framework in this example. To achieve integration with such frameworks, additional work is required: You would need to understand how the API works and design your code to fit within it. 

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>

typedef struct {
    pthread_mutex_t mutex;
    pthread_cond_t condition;
    unsigned long count;
} Semaphore;

void sema_init(Semaphore *s, int value) {
    pthread_mutex_init(&s->mutex, NULL);
    pthread_cond_init(&s->condition, NULL);
    s->count = value;
}

void sema_wait(Semaphore *s) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&s->mutex);

    while (s->count == 0)  // Handle spurious wake-ups.
        pthread_cond_wait(&s->condition, &s->mutex);

    s->count--;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&s->mutex);
}

void sema_signal(Semaphore *s) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&s->mutex);
    s->count++;

    if (s->count == 1)
        pthread_cond_broadcast(&s->condition);  // Wake up all the waiting threads.

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&s->mutex);
}
```

This code provides a simple semaphore that can be used to synchronize access to shared resources from multiple threads in C. It uses a mutex and a condition variable for thread synchronization, which are POSIX standard mechanisms for handling concurrent operations. Note that these mechanisms will work best when the number of threads is not excessively high, as they can lead to contention issues with large numbers of threads. However, for up to 1000 threads, this approach should be sufficient.
"]

["Here's a basic outline for your C++ module using the forward list container provided by the Standard Template Library (STL). Note that the implementation of external logging service is left as a placeholder. The code also includes some simple error handling mechanism and thread safety with mutex.

```cpp
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>
#include <mutex>

class MyForwardList {
    std::forward_list<std::string> list;
    std::mutex mtx;  // for thread safety
public:
    void insertAfterOrAppend(const std::string& target, const std::string& replacement) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);  // ensure thread-safe operation

        bool found = false;
        for (auto it = list.begin(); it != list.end(); ++it) {
            if (*it == target) {
                list.insert_after(it, replacement);  // insert after the first occurrence of target string
                found = true;
                break;
            }
        }

        if (!found) {
            list.push_front(replacement);  // append at the end if target not found
        }
    }
};
```
Please be noted that the external logging service needs to be properly implemented as it's currently missing in this code block due to its complexity and dependencies on specific libraries or services. Also, unit tests are left out for brevity and should be added according to your specific requirements. This code also does not include error handling mechanisms for potential failures in the external logging service, which would depend greatly on how you want to handle such cases (e.g., retries, fallback strategies, etc.). You can add appropriate try-catch blocks around calls to `externalLoggingService` and implement suitable error handling code.
