['Here's a basic implementation of what you might need. This function assumes that the clinic is identified by its ID and has methods for fetching the latest details from an external API and updating itself in the database. The use of semaphores allows for control over shared resources to prevent deadlocks, as well as proper management of database locks for thread-safety:

```php
class Clinic {
    private $id;

    // ... rest of your class here ... 

    public function updateFromAPI($api) {
        try {
            $latestDetails = $api->fetchLatestClinicDetails($this->id);

            if (!$latestDetails) {
                throw new Exception("Invalid data received from the API.");
            }

            // This operation is thread-safe due to the use of a semaphore
            $sem = sem_get(ftok(__FILE__, 'u'), 1); 
            if (sem_acquire($sem)) {
                $this->status = $latestDetails['status'];
                $this->lastModifiedIP = $latestDetails['ip'];
                $this->lastModifiedDate = date('Y-m-d H:i:s');
                $this->save();  // save the clinic object to the database
                sem_release($sem);
            } else {
                throw new Exception("Unable to acquire lock for updating clinic.");
            }
        } catch (Exception $e) {
            $message = "Error while fetching data from the API or saving to DB: " . $e->getMessage();
            error_log($message);  // log the error

            if (defined('DEBUG') && DEBUG === true) {
                echo $message;  // print error message in debug mode for easier debugging
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This code uses a semaphore to control access to shared resources and ensures that only one process can update the clinic at any given time. The actual implementation of the API class, database operations, and error logging will depend on your specific requirements and setup.
