['Implementing all these features in a single response would be an overly complex task and not feasible in this format. However, I can provide you with a basic skeleton of how to approach this problem using NodeJS, Express, and Redis for caching purposes. 

```js
const express = require('express');
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');
const app = express();
const port = 3000;
const md = require('markdown-it')();
const Redis = require("ioredis");
const redis = new Redis();

app.get('/:filename', async (req, res) => {
    const filename = req.params.filename;

    if(!fs.existsSync(path.join(__dirname, 'markdownfiles/'+filename))){
        return res.status(404).send('File not found');
    }

    let htmlData = await redis.get(filename);

    if (htmlData) {
        console.log("Serving from cache");
        return res.send(htmlData);
    } else {
        fs.readFile(path.join(__dirname, 'markdownfiles/'+filename), 'utf8', function(err, data){
            if (err) throw err;

            console.log("Processing markdown file");
            htmlData = md.render(data);

            redis.set(filename, htmlData).then(() => {
                res.send(htmlData);
            });
        });
    }
});

app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Markdown processor app listening at http://localhost:${port}`)
})
```
This code snippet starts a simple Express server that listens on port 3000 and responds to `GET /:filename` requests with the Markdown file transformed into HTML. It caches the result in Redis, so if the same request is made again it retrieves the cached version from Redis instead of re-processing the markdown.

You'll need to expand this code significantly for your use case, including adding directory changes detection with fs.watch, dealing with large files and implementing a background worker queue, implementing an external metadata API rate limit queuing mechanism, load balancing, persistent metadata storage using a database, message broker integration, auto-scaling options, error handling and fallback strategies, testing, among other features you've asked for. 

Also keep in mind the fact that NodeJS is single threaded but it can handle asynchronous tasks efficiently due to its event loop model which makes it well suited for these kinds of tasks. It would be better if you hire a professional developer or consult with a consultant who has worked on similar projects before. This task cannot be accomplished by a junior developer alone and it would require significant experience in various aspects of web development, NodeJS, Redis, system design etc. 

