["Below is a simple implementation of a Node.js module that provides a streaming API for reading bytes from a specified number of streams. This is a simplified version that doesn't directly integrate with external data sources or third-party services but can be extended as needed. We use the `Readable` stream interface provided by Node.js which supports both text and binary formats:

```javascript
const { Readable } = require('stream');
const fs = require('fs');
const winston = require('winston'); // for logging

// Set up a logger
const logger = winston.createLogger({
  level: 'info',
  format: winston.format.json(),
  defaultMeta: { service: 'data-streaming-service' },
  transports: [new winston.transports.Console()],
});

class DataStream extends Readable {
  constructor(source, options = {}) {
    super(options);
    this.source = source;
  }

  _read(size) {
    let chunkData;

    try {
      // read data from the source (could be a file or any other stream).
      fs.read(this.source, size, (err, bytesRead, buffer) => {
        if (err) {
          logger.log('error', 'Error reading data: ', err);
          this.emit('error', new Error('Failed to read from source'));
        } else {
          chunkData = buffer;
          // Push the data into the stream, which will invoke _read again when more data is needed.
          if (!this.push(chunkData)) {
            logger.log('error', 'Error pushing chunk data: ', err);
            this.emit('error', new Error('Failed to push chunk data'));
          }
        }
      });
    } catch (err) {
      logger.log('error', 'Error in reading from source: ', err);
      this.emit('error', new Error('Unexpected error'));
    }
  }
}
```

This is a basic implementation and does not handle concurrency, high load or failure scenarios directly. To extend it for concurrent handling you could use `cluster` module in Node.js to create child processes that can share server ports but also for handling failure scenarios, try/catch blocks should be added within the read method (not shown above) to catch and log any errors.

In terms of unit tests, a test framework like Jest or Mocha can be used to write test cases to cover all edge cases, including network failures and unexpected data formats:

```javascript
const { describe, it } = require('mocha');
const assert = require('assert');

describe('DataStream', () => {
  it('should read the correct amount of bytes from a source', (done) => {
    const stream = new DataStream(source);
