["Here is a simplified example of how you can start implementing an AsyncTaskManager in C++ using std::async and std::future, while it does not consider all your requirements mentioned above like error handling, distributed task execution, leader election etc., but this gives a basic idea about how to handle tasks asynchronously:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <functional>
#include <thread>
#include <future>

class AsyncTaskManager {
    std::vector<std::future<void>> futures;
public:
    ~AsyncTaskManager() {
        for (auto& f : futures) {
            if(f.valid()) {
                f.wait();
            }
        }
    }

    template <typename F, typename... Args>
    void submit_task(F&& f, Args&&... args) {
        auto task = std::bind(std::forward<F>(f), std::forward<Args>(args)...);
        futures.push_back(std::async(std::launch::async, task));
    }
};
```
In this example, the class `AsyncTaskManager` has a vector of futures that are used to store the results of the submitted tasks. When a task is submitted with `submit_task()`, it's bound with any provided arguments and launched asynchronously using std::async. The future returned by std::async is stored in the futures vector.

This is very basic implementation. You need to enhance this according to your requirements such as managing high concurrency, handling external system dependencies, implementing adaptive scheduling etc. 

Also, it's important to note that C++ doesn't come with a built-in thread pool, you would have to implement one on your own or use third party libraries for managing threads like Boost.Asio or Intel TBB which support efficient task scheduling and concurrency control.
"]

