["Your task involves implementing a full-fledged HTTP client in Rust, which can be quite complex and lengthy. Here is an overview of how you could start building such a system with some libraries that might be useful:

```rust
use reqwest::Client;
use std::time::Duration;
use tokio::time::delay_for;
use futures::future::FutureExt;

struct HttpClient {
    client: Client,
}

impl Default for HttpClient {
    fn default() -> Self {
        let client = Client::builder().timeout(Duration::from_secs(30)).build().unwrap();
        Self { client }
    }
}

impl HttpClient {
    pub async fn get(&self, url: &str) -> Result<String, reqwest::Error> {
        let response = self.client.get(url).send().await?;

        if response.status().is_success() {
            Ok(response.text().await?)
        } else {
            Err(reqwest::Error::Status(response))
        }
    }

    pub async fn post(&self, url: &str, body: &str) -> Result<String, reqwest::Error> {
        let response = self.client.post(url).body(body).send().await?;

        if response.status().is_success() {
            Ok(response.text().await?)
        } else {
            Err(reqwest::Error::Status(response))
        }
    }
}
```

This is a very simple HTTP client implementation using `reqwest` for Rust, which supports asynchronous operations and integrates with the futures system. You can extend this to fit your requirements. 

