["Creating a complete library that follows all of these criteria would be quite extensive, so here's an outline for how you might approach this task in C using pthreads for thread safety and reusing memory as much as possible. I will not include the full implementation due to its complexity, but this should give you a good starting point:

```C
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    /* fields */
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct Node {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* data;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
Node* head = NULL;
int totalNodes = 0;

void insert(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* data) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    ++totalNodes;
    Node* newNode = (Node*)malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if (!newNode) {
        /* Out of memory. Handle gracefully here */
        --totalNodes;
        return;
    }
    newNode->data = data;
    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* remove() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    if (head == NULL) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        return NULL;
    }
    --totalNodes;
    Node* temp = head;
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* data = temp->data;
    head = head->next;
    free(temp);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return data;
}
```
This code implements a linked list in C, with each node holding a `NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T` structure. It uses a mutex to ensure thread safety when modifying the list (for example, inserting or removing nodes). If the system runs out of memory while trying to allocate for a new node, it decrements the total number of nodes and returns without doing anything else.

However, this code doesn't handle integration issues with external systems, data timeouts, concurrent access from multiple threads, optimizing for both time and space complexity, or error handling strategies for out-of-memory conditions. It also does not include a logging framework. 

A full implementation would need to consider these factors and potentially add further features such as automated testing, documentation generation with Doxygen, and more complex memory management depending on the specific requirements of your project.
