['Here is a general skeleton of how you can start to design your C library. Keep in mind that this code will have to be developed into a complete working library that meets all your requirements, which might involve hundreds or thousands of lines of code depending on complexity. This example also does not handle any of the error checking and exception handling you would need for real-world use.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
  // Structure definition goes here
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct node {
  NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* data;
  struct node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
int count = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock;

void add(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* element) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

  Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
  newNode->data = element;
  newNode->next = head;
  head = newNode;
  count++;

  pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* get(int index) {
  if (index < 0 || index >= count) return NULL;

  pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

  Node* current = head;
  for (int i = 0; i < index; i++) {
    current = current->next;
  }

  NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* result = current->data;

  pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

  return result;
}

void remove(int index) {
  if (index < 0 || index >= count) return;

  pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

  Node* current = head;
  Node* previous = NULL;

  for (int i = 0; i < index; i++) {
    previous = current;
    current = current->next;
  }

  if (previous != NULL) {
    previous->next = current->next;
  } else {
    head = current->next;
  }

  free(current);
  count--;

  pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```

This is a basic singly linked list implementation. You can add more methods to search, sort, etc. Also note that thread safety is achieved with a mutex lock, which ensures only one thread can access the data at a time.

Please consult a professional developer or hire a development team for this task as it requires a significant amount of work and knowledge of C programming, concurrency issues, error handling, integration issues, testing, performance analysis, memory management, encryption, etc. It is also important to note that not all functions have been implemented here, but the basic structure and thread safety principles are covered.
']

