["Here's a basic JavaScript implementation of `executeTestModules` based on the described requirements. 
Please note that this is just a basic implementation, and might need further optimization or modification depending on the exact requirements:

```javascript
async function executeTestModules(moduleName, testFiles, dependencyManager, blueslip) {
    // Ensure dependencies are loaded
    for (let file of testFiles) {
        let module = require(file);
        try {
            await module.loadDependencies(dependencyManager);
        } catch (e) {
            blueslip.logError(`Failed to load dependencies for ${file} in module ${moduleName}: ${e}`);
        }
    }

    // Run tests concurrently with a limit on concurrency
    const MAX_CONCURRENT = 5;
    let runningTests = 0;
    let index = 0;
    while (index < testFiles.length) {
        if (runningTests < MAX_CONCURRENT && !testFiles[index].isRunning) {
            try {
                await executeTest(testFiles[index]);
            } catch (e) {
                blueslip.logError(`Failed to run test ${testFiles[index]} in module ${moduleName}: ${e}`);
            } finally {
                runningTests--;
            }
        } else {
            await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 10)); // Avoid blocking the event loop
        }

        index++;
        if (index === testFiles.length) {
            index = 0;
        }
    }
}

async function executeTest(testFile) {
    testFile.isRunning = true;
    try {
        await testFile.run();
    } catch (e) {
        throw new Error(`Failed to run test ${testFile}: ${e}`);
    } finally {
        testFile.isRunning = false;
    }
}
```
This code loads the dependencies for each test file before running them concurrently, limiting the number of tests that can be run at once to prevent overloading the server. It also handles errors gracefully and keeps track of which tests are currently being run so they can't interfere with each other or themselves. 

However, this implementation is quite basic and might need adjustments based on your application's specific requirements, such as how you handle dependencies, what kind of concurrency control you want to use, etc. 

Also, it doesn't provide any feedback about the progress or status of tests, which was part of your user experience requirement. If you want real-time updates during test execution, you might need to add code for that and make sure it doesn't interfere with the overall performance and concurrency management. 

Finally, this implementation assumes that each `testFile` object has a `run()` method for running tests and a `loadDependencies(dependencyManager)` method for loading dependencies. You would need to ensure that these methods exist in your test files and work as expected.
