    // Assume GetHash is a method that hashes the password using Argon2 and encodes it in Base64.
    public async Task<string> ProcessCredentialsAsync(User user) {
        var client = _httpClientFactory.CreateClient("authentication");

        string hashedPassword = GetHash(user.Password);

        // Assume the endpoint requires a password of 'password' parameter and username in the body.
        var content = new StringContent(JsonConvert.SerializeObject(new {username = user.Username, password = hashedPassword}), Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

        var response = await Policy
            .Handle<HttpRequestException>()
            .OrResult<HttpResponseMessage>(r => !r.IsSuccessStatusCode)
            .WaitAndRetryAsync(3, retryAttempt =>  TimeSpan.FromSeconds(Math.Pow(2, retryAttempt)))
            .ExecuteAsync(() => client.PostAsync("http://external-api/login", content));

        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
    }
}
```

The `IHttpClientFactory` is used to create a named HTTP client which can be easily swapped out in testing or for different environments. Polly's `WaitAndRetryAsync` policy will retry the request up to 3 times with exponential backoff, and it will only retry on network failures or when the response status code indicates an unsuccessful request.

This is a very basic example and does not include any of the other requirements you've listed such as load testing, performance optimization, rate limiting, secure hashing passwords, etc. Each of these would need to be added in their own layer or service with more specific error handling and logging. Please note that this code also assumes that there are some kind of `User` class already defined elsewhere. 

