['Here is the skeleton code for `RandomSequences` library:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <random>
#include <mutex>
#include <stdexcept>

namespace RandomSequences {

class MTGenerator {
    private:
        std::vector<std::mt19937_64> generators;
        std::vector<std::uniform_real_distribution<double>> distributions;
        std::mutex mtx;

    public:
        // Constructor
        MTGenerator(int num_sequences) {
            if (num_sequences <= 0) throw std::invalid_argument("Number of sequences must be positive.");

            generators.reserve(num_sequences);
            distributions.resize(num_sequences, std::uniform_real_distribution<double>(0.0, 1.0));

            for (int i = 0; i < num_sequences; ++i) {
                generators.emplace_back(std::mt19937_64(i));
            }
        }

        // Destructor
        ~MTGenerator() {}

        double get_random(int sequence_index) {
            if (sequence_index < 0 || sequence_index >= generators.size()) 
                throw std::out_of_range("Sequence index out of range.");

            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx); // thread safety
            return distributions[sequence_index](generators[sequence_index]);
        }

        void reset_sequence(int sequence_index) {
            if (sequence_index < 0 || sequence_index >= generators.size()) 
                throw std::out_of_range("Sequence index out of range.");

            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx); // thread safety
            generators[sequence_index].seed(sequence_index);
        }

        void add_sequences(int num_new_sequences) {
            if (num_new_sequences <= 0) throw std::invalid_argument("Number of new sequences must be positive.");

            int start = generators.size();
            distributions.reserve(start + num_new_sequences);
            for (int i = 0; i < num_new_sequences; ++i) {
                generators.emplace_back(std::mt19937_64(start+i));
                distributions.push_back(std::uniform_real_distribution<double>(0.0, 1.0));
            }
        }
};
} // end of namespace RandomSequences
```
This is a basic implementation and does not include the features to interact with external systems or handle system dependencies. More complex error handling, logging, and optimization strategies will be needed for production-level code. For instance, you might want to consider using smart pointers instead of raw pointers, implementing RAII principles for managing resources, and so on.
']

["Here is an outline for implementing a DynamicMicroservicesOrchestrationEngine in Java. This code would be part of a much larger project, and this does not cover all requirements, it's just the skeleton of how you might structure your engine to manage and orchestrate microservices. You will need to add detailed implementations for each feature according to your specific needs.

```java
public class DynamicMicroservicesOrchestrationEngine {
    // Service discovery, health monitoring, load balancing, etc. objects 
    private ServiceDiscovery serviceDiscovery;
    private HealthMonitoringService healthMonitoringService;
    private LoadBalancer loadBalancer;

    public DynamicMicroservicesOrchestrationEngine() {
        // Initialize services and load balancers as required
        this.serviceDiscovery = new ServiceDiscovery();
        this.healthMonitoringService = new HealthMonitoringService(this.serviceDiscovery);
        this.loadBalancer = new LoadBalancer(this.serviceDiscovery, this.healthMonitoringService);
    }

    public void registerService(Service service) {
        // Register a service with the orchestration engine and health monitoring service
        this.serviceDiscovery.registerService(service);
        this.healthMonitoringService.monitorService(service);
    }

    public void deregisterService(String serviceId) {
        // Deregister a service from the orchestration engine and health monitoring service
        Service service = this.serviceDiscovery.getServiceById(serviceId);

        if (service != null) {
            this.serviceDiscovery.deregisterService(service);
            this.healthMonitoringService.stopMonitoringService(service);
        }
    }

    public void handleRequest(Request request) {
        // Handle a client request by selecting a service from the load balancer and forwarding the request to it
        Service targetService = this.loadBalancer.selectService();

        if (targetService != null && targetService.isHealthy()) {
            try {
                Response response = targetService.forwardRequest(request);

                // Handle response as needed
            } catch (Exception e) {
                this.healthMonitoringService.reportFailedAttempt(targetService, request, e);
                // Retry logic goes here
            }
        } else {
            // Handle case where no healthy service is available
        }
    }

    public void handleEvent(String eventType, Object data) {
        // Handle an event by selecting a service from the load balancer and forwarding it to it
        Service targetService = this.loadBalancer.selectService();

        if (targetService != null && targetService.isHealthy()) {
            try {
                targetService.processEvent(eventType, data);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                this.healthMonitoringService.reportFailedAttemptToProcessEvent(targetService, eventType, data, e);
                // Retry logic goes here
            }
        } else {
            // Handle case where no healthy service is available
        }
    }
}
```

This is a simplified version of what your orchestration engine might look like. The details and complexity will greatly depend on the specific requirements of your project, such as the need for fault tolerance and rate limiting strategies, advanced load balancing techniques, and integration with external monitoring tools. Please consider adding more features according to your needs.
"]

