['Sorry, your request is quite complex and involves multiple parts, including concurrent programming, cloud integration, distributed system design, AI/ML model implementation, etc. 

Here's a brief example how to implement multithreaded file transfer in Rust. This will be just a basic skeleton code for your problem which you can expand upon:

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::Read;
use std::net::TcpStream;
use std::thread;
use std::time::Duration;
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};
use std::vec::Vec;

static NUM_THREADS: usize = 8;
static CHUNK_SIZE: usize = 4096;
static HOST: &str = "localhost";
static PORT: &str = "12345";

type Progress = Arc<AtomicUsize>;
fn start_transfer(file_name: String, progress: Progress) {
    let addr = format::<String>(!HOST, !PORT);
    let stream = TcpStream::connect(&*addr).unwrap();

    // Open the file
    let mut file = match File::open(&file_name) {
        Ok(file) => file,
        Err(_) => return println::<String>("Error opening file"),
    };

    let mut buffer = vec![0; CHUNK_SIZE];

    loop {
        match file.read(&mut buffer) {
            Ok(size) if size == 0 => break,
            Ok(_nread) => {
                stream.write(&buffer).unwrap();
                let old_progress = progress.fetch_add(CHUNK_SIZE, Ordering::SeqCst);
                println::<usize>(format::<String, usize>("Progress: {}%", (old_progress * 100 / file.metadata().unwrap().len()) as usize));
            },
            Err(_error) => {
                return println::<String>("Error reading file");
            }
        };
    }
}

fn main() {
    let progress = Arc::new(AtomicUsize::new(0));

    // Assume the file name is passed as a command line argument
    let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();
    if args.len() < 2 {
        println::<String>("File name not provided");
        return;
    }

    let mut children = vec![];

    for _ in 0..NUM_THREADS {
        let progress = progress.clone();

        let child = thread::spawn(move || {
            start_transfer(args[1].clone(), progress);
        });

        children.push(child);
    }

    for child in children {
        let _ = child.join().unwrap();
    }
}
```
This code starts multiple threads that read a file and send its content to the server. The number of concurrent transfers is controlled by the NUM_THREADS constant, and each thread reads CHUNK_SIZE bytes from the file at once. It also reports progress on the console. 

Please note that this code doesn't include all the features you requested (e.g., retries on failure, dynamic resource allocation, etc.), as they would significantly increase the complexity of the code and probably require a full rewrite in Rust for better performance and safety. You might want to consider using an existing solution or writing this part in another language that has more support for distributed systems and concurrency.
']

