["This is a complex task that involves multiple aspects of software design, and writing full code for it would be too large and lengthy to fit into this format. However, I can certainly outline some fundamental pieces that will aid you in creating such an application.

To interact with external APIs, consider using libraries like libcurl or RestClient++. For handling different data formats from third-party API's, you might want to use JSON library like jsoncpp or RapidJSON.

For load balancing and concurrency control, consider using thread pools, futures and promises in C++11 or newer.

To manage error recovery and system availability, consider implementing a circuit breaker pattern where requests are failed fast when the system is experiencing failures. You might also want to use techniques like bulkheads, which isolate parts of your application from each other so that if one part fails, it does not impact others.

Here's an example of how you could define classes for a producer and consumer model in C++:

```cpp
#include <queue>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

// This is the data type that will be passed around between different threads.
struct Data {
    // ... fill this out as per your requirements ...
};

class ProducerConsumerQueue{
public:
    void push(Data const& data){
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);
        buffer_.push(data);
        lock.unlock();
        cond_.notify_one();
    }

    bool empty() const {
        return buffer_.empty();
    }

    bool tryPop(Data& poppedValue){
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);
        if(buffer_.empty())
            return false;
        poppedValue=buffer_.front();
        buffer_.pop();
        return true;
    }

    void waitAndPop(Data& poppedValue){
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);
        while(buffer_.empty())
            cond_.wait(lock);
        poppedValue=buffer_.front();
        buffer_.pop();
    }
private:
    std::queue<Data> buffer_;
    mutable std::mutex m;
    std::condition_variable cond_;
};
```
In this example, `ProducerConsumerQueue` is a thread-safe queue that can be used to pass data between different threads. The `push()` function adds an item to the queue and notifies waiting threads that there's new data available. The `waitAndPop()` function waits until there is data in the queue before popping it off.

For system configuration, consider using a library like libconfig for reading config files or consider using a database like SQLite or LevelDB if you need to store more complex data structures. 

