['This is a complex task that requires detailed design and implementation. Here's a simplified example using `pthread` for thread safety and a dynamically resizable array implemented as a linked list. Note that this does not include all the functionalities you requested such as network retries, profiling and error handling, distributed logging nodes and centralized aggregation points etc., due to its complexity and the limitations of text responses in this platform.

Please make sure to include appropriate headers and compile with -pthread for thread support.

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct LogEvent {
    time_t timestamp; // You might want to use uint64_t here for more precision
    int eventType;
} LogEvent;

// A simple linked list implementation for storing the logs.
typedef struct Node {
    LogEvent* logEvent;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL; // Head of the linked list.
pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

// Function to add a new log event to the end of the list.
void AddLogEvent(int eventType) {
    LogEvent* newLogEvent = malloc(sizeof(LogEvent)); // Allocate memory for new log event.

    if (newLogEvent == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to allocate memory for new log event.\n");
        return;
    }

    newLogEvent->timestamp = time(NULL);
    newLogEvent->eventType = eventType;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex); // Lock the mutex.

    Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node)); // Allocate memory for new node.
    if (newNode == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to allocate memory for new node.\n");
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex); // Unlock the mutex before return.
        return;
    }

    newNode->logEvent = newLogEvent;
    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex); // Unlock the mutex after adding a new log event.
}

// Function to retrieve all logs within a given timestamp range.
void GetLogsInRange(time_t start, time_t end) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    Node* currentNode = head;
    while (currentNode != NULL) {
        if (currentNode->logEvent->timestamp >= start && currentNode->logEvent->timestamp <= end) {
            printf("Timestamp: %ld, Event Type: %d\n", currentNode->logEvent->timestamp, currentNode->logEvent->eventType);
        }
        currentNode = currentNode->next;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}

// Function to clear logs older than a given retention period.
void ClearOldLogs(time_t retentionPeriod) {
    time_t threshold = time(NULL) - retentionPeriod;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    Node* currentNode = head;
    Node* previousNode = NULL;

    while (currentNode != NULL) {
        if (currentNode->logEvent->timestamp < threshold) {
            // Remove the node from the list.
            if (previousNode == NULL) {
                head = currentNode->next;
            } else {
                previousNode->next = currentNode->next;
            }

            free(currentNode->logEvent);
            Node* temp = currentNode;
            currentNode = currentNode->next;
            free(temp); // Free the node.
        } else {
            previousNode = currentNode;
            currentNode = currentNode->next;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}
``` 

You might also want to consider using other data structures or libraries such as glib's GAsyncQueue for efficient concurrent access and management of large amounts of log events, or libcurl for API calls. Also, you should consider using a more advanced profiling tool than printf for performance analysis and optimization.
']

["Here is an outline of how you might tackle this task using C99 standard. 

