["Here is a basic implementation of the `executeModuleTests` function in JavaScript. Note that this code does not include all the features mentioned above due to its complexity. For example, it assumes that testRunner is available globally and doesn't handle version mismatches, API changes or deprecations in the testing framework over time. Also, it doesn't include error handling for edge cases or unexpected input. 

```javascript
const semaphore = require('semaphore');
const {promisify} = require('util');
const sleep = promisify(setTimeout);
let testSemaphore = semaphore(10); // Create a semaphore with a concurrency limit of 10

async function executeModuleTests(modulePath, tests) {
    let module;

    try {
        module = require(modulePath);
    } catch (e) {
        console.error(`Failed to load module at path: ${modulePath}`);
        throw e;
    }

    const results = [];

    for (let test of tests) {
        await testSemaphore.take(); // Acquire a semaphore

        try {
            let result = await testRunner(test, module);
            results.push(result);
        } catch (e) {
            console.error(`Failed to execute test: ${test}`);
            throw e; // Pass the exception upwards
        } finally {
            testSemaphore.leave(); // Release the semaphore
        }
    }

    return results;
}
```
This code uses a semaphore to control concurrency, limiting the number of tests that can be executed at once. It also uses try/catch blocks around each test execution to handle errors and ensure that every semaphore is released even if an error occurs. This function assumes that `testRunner` is a function that takes two parameters: a test description and the module under test, and returns a promise that resolves when the test completes. 

Please adapt this code according to your own needs. Also remember to handle exceptions properly in production level code. For example, you might want to implement some form of exponential backoff or retry logic for network errors, etc. This will ensure that your application remains stable and responsive even if external dependencies fail occasionally. 

Remember to install `semaphore` library using npm:
```bash
npm install --save semaphore
```
