```java
// Java-Based Background Task Processor (Java)
import java.util.concurrent.*;

class UnionFind {
    /*...*/
}

public class DisjointPairProcessor implements Callable<Integer[]> {

    private int[] numbers;

    public DisjointPairProcessor(int[] numbers){
        this.numbers = numbers;
    }

    @Override
    public Integer[] call() throws Exception {
        UnionFind uf = new UnionFind();

        for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length-1; i++) {
            if(!uf.isConnected(numbers[i], numbers[i+1])){
                uf.union(numbers[i], numbers[i+1]);
            }
        }

        return uf.getDisjointPairs();
    }
}

public class BackgroundTaskProcessor {

    private ExecutorService executor;

    public BackgroundTaskProcessor(int nThreads){
        this.executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(nThreads);
    }

    public Future<Integer[]> processDisjointPairs(int[] numbers){
        return executor.submit(new DisjointPairProcessor(numbers));
    }
}
```
In this example, I've used a simplified version of Union-Find and authentication provider for the sake of readability. You might need to implement actual versions that work in your specific context (e.g., using a database or calling an external service). In real implementation, you would also need to handle multi-factor authentication, retry mechanism with exponential backoff, OAuth 2.0 integration, etc.
