However, I will provide a simple example of how you might start with designing and implementing an inventory management system microservice in Java:

```java
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.util.List;

@RestController
public class InventoryController {
    private final InventoryRepository repository;

    InventoryController(InventoryRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @GetMapping("/inventories")
    List<Inventory> all() {
        return repository.findAll();
    }

    @PostMapping("/inventories")
    Inventory newInventory(@RequestBody Inventory newInventory) {
        return repository.save(newInventory);
    }

    @GetMapping("/inventories/{id}")
    Inventory one(@PathVariable Long id) throws InventoryNotFoundException {
        return repository.findById(id).orElseThrow(() -> new InventoryNotFoundException(id));
    }

    @PutMapping("/inventories/{id}")
    Inventory replaceInventory(@RequestBody Inventory newInventory, @PathVariable Long id) {
        return repository.findById(id).map(inventory -> {
            inventory.setItemName(newInventory.getItemName());
            inventory.setQuantity(newInventory.getQuantity());
            return repository.save(inventory);
        }).orElseGet(() -> {
            newInventory.setId(id);
            return repository.save(newInventory);
        });
    }

    @DeleteMapping("/inventories/{id}")
    void deleteInventory(@PathVariable Long id) {
        repository.deleteById(id);
    }
}
```
This code represents a simple REST API for an inventory management system. It uses Spring Data JPA and H2 database to interact with the data layer, which is common in Java-based microservices architectures. The API provides basic CRUD operations (Create, Read, Update, Delete) on a 'Inventory' resource.
