["Here is a high level overview of how you might structure the code to accomplish this task in C. Please note that full implementation may be extensive based on your specific requirements and constraints.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU {
    int RNTI;
    int RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG;
    int CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct Node {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data;
    struct Node* next;
} Node_t;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
Node_t* head = NULL;

void* fetch_from_external_source() {
    // Implement your logic here to fetch data from external source.
}

NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T get_ue_info(int rnti) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    Node_t* node = head;

    // Search for the node with matching RNTI value
    while (node != NULL && node->data.RNTI < rnti) {
        node = node->next;
    }

    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T result;
    if (node != NULL && node->data.RNTI == rnti) {
        // If matching RNTI is found, copy data to result struct and release lock
        result = node->data;
    } else {
        // If no match found, fetch from external source and insert into list in ascending order
        NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T new_data = fetch_from_external_source();

        Node_t* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node_t));
        newNode->data = new_data;
        newNode->next = node->next;
        if (node != NULL) {
            node->next = newNode;
        } else {
            head = newNode;
        }

        result = new_data;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return result;
}
```
In the above code, `get_ue_info` is a thread-safe function that fetches the UE information for a given RNTI. If the required data is already in the list, it's simply returned. If not, the external source is contacted to retrieve the data and then added to the correct position in the linked list (in ascending order of RNTI).

Please note that this code does not handle out-of-memory conditions or network latency issues as these would require a significantly more complex design including thread synchronization, error handling, logging and monitoring mechanisms. Implementing such functionality will depend on your specific requirements and constraints. For example, if your external data source is unreliable and prone to delays or corrupted data, you might need to include error checking code in `fetch_from_external_source` that retries failed requests with exponential backoff and circuit breaker patterns. Similarly, efficient garbage collection mechanisms would be necessary for managing memory usage when the linked list is storing large amounts of data. You may also wish to implement a caching strategy to reduce frequency of external data fetches and improve response times.
