['Here is a high level outline of how you could approach this problem in Java, given the complex nature of the task. This solution does not cover all aspects and is meant to illustrate the general structure of your codebase.

```java
public interface SchemaAdapter {
    void adaptSchema(Object object);
}

public class DynamicEventStreamingPlatform {
    private final ExecutorService executor;
    private final List<Consumer> consumers = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    public DynamicEventStreamingPlatform() {
        this.executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());
    }

    public void registerConsumer(Consumer consumer) {
        consumers.add(consumer);
    }

    public void publishEvent(Object event, SchemaAdapter adapter) {
        executor.submit(() -> {
            for (Consumer consumer : consumers) {
                try {
                    adapter.adaptSchema(event);
                    consumeEventSafely(consumer, event);
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    handleException(e);
                }
            }
        });
    }

    private void consumeEventSafely(Consumer consumer, Object event) {
        try {
            consumer.consume(event);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            handleException(e);
        }
    }

    // Load state object from file using ObjectInputStream safely handling exceptions 
    public static MoSTState loadStateFromFile(String filename) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        try (ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream(filename))) {
            Object obj = in.readObject();
            if (obj instanceof MoSTState) {
                return (MoSTState) obj;
            }
        }
        return null;
    }

    private void handleException(Exception e) {
        // Handle exception, log error or propagate it further
    }
}
```
In the `publishEvent` method we are submitting a task to an executor service. This enables us to process multiple events concurrently. Each consumer is notified of the event and if any exceptions occur, they are handled by the `handleException` method.

The `SchemaAdapter` interface is used to adapt the schema of incoming events as required. The specific implementation would depend on your needs. 

In a real-world scenario, you would likely want more sophisticated load balancing and backpressure mechanisms, potentially involving message queuing systems like Apache Kafka or RabbitMQ, and distributed consensus algorithms for conflict resolution and eventual consistency. You might also consider using a library to handle many of the complexities involved in creating such a system.
